#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105475 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b13a2e9-d7e8-51a3-b452-0594858dd0d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given the First Term and Another Term"}] :question/id #uuid "e7b51055-36f2-552b-817e-3632cf0446b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12924" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given that the first term is $-1$ and the eighth term is $13$." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is $a_1=-1$, so we just need to figure out the common difference $d$.\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_1=-1$ and $a_8=13$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{8} - a_{1}}{8 - 1} \\\\\n&= (13 - (-1))/(8 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{7} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] & = -1 + (n-1) (2) \\\\[3pt] & = -1 + 2n - 2 \\\\[3pt] & = 2n - 3. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105476 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23171184-edaf-560e-a5a6-fd7f0be2f6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Consecutive Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "c7bb25e3-0f81-5290-970d-6785ecbc921c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12925" :question/problem "The fifth and sixth terms of an arithmetic sequence are $46$ and $50$, respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$.\n\nSince the terms $a_5=46$ and $a_6=50$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{6} - a_{5} \\\\\n&= 50 - 46 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_5=46$, to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_5$ is $4$ jumps after $a_1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_5 &= a_1 + 4d \\\\[3pt] 46 &= a_1 + 4\\cdot 4 \\\\[3pt] 46 &= a_1 +16 \\\\[3pt] 46-16 &= a_1 \\\\[3pt] a_1&= 30. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] & = 30 + (n-1) \\cdot 4 \\\\[3pt] & =30 + 4n -4 \\\\[3pt] & = 4n +26. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97cdef9-0f97-5f2e-a168-2ea62e08a7f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "e564d562-6dd0-5736-a539-63b6c6592e81" :question/math-academy-id "e-12927" :question/problem "An arithmetic sequence has the $3$rd term $a_{3}=9$ and the $8$th term is $a_{8}=69$. Find a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out what the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$ are.\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_{8}=69$ and $a_{3}=9$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{8} - a_{3}}{8 - 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{69 - 9}{8 - 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{60}{5} \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_{3}=9$, to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after $a_1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{3} &= a_{1} + 2d \\\\\n9 &= a_{1} + 2 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n9 &= a_{1} + 24 \\\\\na_{1} &= -15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= -15 + (n - 1) \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&=-15 + 12n - 12 \\\\\n&= 12n - 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
